  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gusty winds this afternoon, especially across \nWestern NY.\n\nA tight pressure gradient across the Great Lakes this afternoon as a \npotent mid level trough and associated cold front approach from the \nnorthwest. KBUF VWP showed 35 to 40 knots in the 2K to 4K layer with \nbreaks of sunshine helping to mix much of the stronger winds down\nto the surface. Peak gusts in the 40 to 45 mph range northeast \nof Lake Erie from the Niagara Frontier to near Rochester will \ncontinue this afternoon, with gusts in the 20 to 30 mph range \ntoday.\n\nIt will remain windy tonight through Monday morning, with boundary\nlayer flow gradually veering to the WNW overnight. Gusts of 25 to\n35 mph will be common across the entire region, with some 40 mph\ngusts possible across the higher terrain of the Southern Tier \nand Finger Lakes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool and unsettled with periods of lake enhanced \nrain showers through Monday morning.\n\nA progressive and potent mid level trough will dig from the upper \nGreat Lakes tonight to New England by Monday, with an associated \ncold front crossing the eastern Great Lakes tonight. The airmass is \ncool enough to support a lake response ahead of the cold front. A \nfew lake effect rain showers should develop east/northeast of the \nlakes by early this evening as forcing and synoptic scale moisture \nincrease. Initial development will be near Buffalo and Watertown on \nWSW flow.\n\nBoundary layer flow will veer to the WNW tonight and NW by Monday \nmorning, carrying lake effect and lake enhanced rain showers \nsoutheast of the lakes. Synoptic scale forcing will be strong \nenough to support scattered to numerous showers even outside of \nlake effect areas overnight through early Monday. Lake enhancement\nwill bring steadier rain and higher QPF east and southeast of the\nlakes, with the highest rain amounts across the Tug Hill and \nOswego County. A few locations may pick up 0.50\" to 0.75\" of rain,\nwith the steady translation of lake effect bands southward in \nveering wind profiles preventing any higher totals. Boundary \nlayer temperatures stay warm enough for all liquid, although a \nfew locations may see some graupel or small hail in convective \nlake effect showers.\n\nShowers will continue through Monday morning, especially southeast \nof the lakes from ongoing lake enhancement and upslope flow. The mid \nlevel trough and associated deeper moisture will then pull away in \nthe afternoon, with showers gradually ending from west to east. The \nlast of the showers across Central NY and the higher terrain of the \nNorth Country will mostly end by mid to late afternoon.",
